{"assets":["/statement-assets/0af0ef127aa0b0c9ab1769619f83f3c12f2193007366cf7fc328dc0db2d26a8b.png"],"contentKind":"statement","contestId":"1764","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"attrs":{"level":1,"role":"title"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"D. Doremy's Pegging Game"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"time_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"time limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"1.5 seconds"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"memory_limit"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"memory limit per test"},{"kind":"text","text":":"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":"512 megabytes"}],"kind":"container"},{"attrs":{"role":"body"},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Doremy has $$$n+1$$$ pegs. There are $$$n$$$ red pegs arranged as vertices of a regular $$$n$$$-sided polygon, numbered from $$$1$$$ to $$$n$$$ in anti-clockwise order. There is also a blue peg of "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"slightly smaller diameter"}],"kind":"container"},{"kind":"text","text":" in the middle of the polygon. A rubber band is stretched around the red pegs."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Doremy is very bored today and has decided to play a game. Initially, she has an empty array $$$a$$$. While the rubber band does not touch the blue peg, she will:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" choose $$$i$$$ ($$$1 \\leq i \\leq n$$$) such that the red peg $$$i$$$ has not been removed; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" remove the red peg $$$i$$$; "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" append $$$i$$$ to the back of $$$a$$$. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Doremy wonders how many possible different arrays $$$a$$$ can be produced by the following process. Since the answer can be big, you are only required to output it modulo $$$p$$$. $$$p$$$ is guaranteed to be a prime number."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/statement-assets/0af0ef127aa0b0c9ab1769619f83f3c12f2193007366cf7fc328dc0db2d26a8b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"game with $$$n=9$$$ and $$$a=[7,5,2,8,3,9,4]$$$ and another game with $$$n=8$$$ and $$$a=[3,4,7,1,8,5,2]$$$"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"input_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The first line contains two integers $$$n$$$ and $$$p$$$ ($$$3 \\leq n \\leq 5000$$$, $$$10^8 \\le p \\le 10^9$$$) — the number of red pegs and the modulo respectively."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"$$$p$$$ is guaranteed to be a prime number."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"output_specification"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output a single integer, the number of different arrays $$$a$$$ that can be produced by the process described above modulo $$$p$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"samples"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Examples"}],"kind":"heading"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n4 100000007\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n16\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample"},"children":[{"attrs":{"role":"sample_input"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Input"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n1145 141919831\n"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"sample_output"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Output"}],"kind":"heading"},{"kind":"code_block","text":"\n105242108\n"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"section"},{"attrs":{"role":"note"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":2},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Note"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In the first test case, $$$n=4$$$, some possible arrays $$$a$$$ that can be produced are $$$[4,2,3]$$$ and $$$[1,4]$$$. However, it is not possible for $$$a$$$ to be $$$[1]$$$ or $$$[1,4,3]$$$."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"section"}],"kind":"document"},"index":"D","problemCode":"1764D","schema":2,"sourceKind":"html","sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/contest/1764/problem/D"}